<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >1) CMD does make crappy drivers.  They are responsible for fUx0ring my drive when Win95 came out.  (I know...Win95 was probably responsible for fUx0ring my drive...but it was the CMD drivers that it didn't get along with)<br>
<br>
2) AMD r0x0rs!  First off, you don't need to overclock them as much because for the last year they've had faster processors than Inthell.  Secondly, they overclock just fine...they just need more cooling (thus, cooled cases).  My last 2 processors have been AMD.  And other than the problem I had with the OEM not sending me a big enough heat sink the first time, I've never had a problem with it.<br>
<br>
3) 3DFX is no longer king.  Nvidia rUlez now.  Just don't buy ASUS boards unless you really need to do video capturing, but don't want a separate board.  I am quite happy with my V6600 Delux Geforce card (I got it before I realized that Geforce 2 would come out...oh....10 minutes later), but it's a pain in the sdd.  I can't run Diablo 2 in 3D mode because for Nvidia, it requires the latest Detonator drivers from Nvidia.  But guess what?  Those don't work with the video capture part of my card.  And for some reason, when I went back to the latest ASUS drivers, now my machine will just hang (I mean completely just stop.  No error, no response, no nothin') when I'm playing games (It happened in EQ, and it happens in D2).<br>
<br>
Anyway, I've had some real horror-show problems with computers, but then again I worked for CompUSA in the Tech Dept for a year or so.  And Nad, if it's taking you 2 weeks to get your video card installed, take it somewhere else.  They're either so backed up with work that they'll do a crappy job, or their techs don't know what they're doing.  I used to do RAM, Video Cards, and battery replacements in under an hour Video Cards, though, I'd always check it in under a diagnostic.  If the card worked with the system, I charged the diagnostic and the install charge, and give it to them in an hour.  If the card didn't work, I'd charge them the diagnostic and tell them they needed a new card.  I was a genius at making $$ for our tech department.  I could charge $70 for time and labor to replace a $5 battery in 15 mins.  For some reason, people are happier paying more if they get their stuff back right away.  But they'll argue about every penny if it takes days.  People would come in with trashed systems, wanting them completely rebuilt.  We'd tell them $70/hr, and they'd say OK.  Then they'd bitch and moan when we wanted to charge them $280 for the 4 hours of honest labor we did when they picked it up a week later.  But they don't blink an eye when you charge $40 diagnostics, $40 installation fee, and $70 for an hours labor to install a hard drive with Win 95 on it in 30 minutes.  Go figure.<br>
<br>
__________________<br>
I love to go down to the schoolyard and watch all the little children jump up and down and run around yelling and screaming...They don't know I'm only using blanks.<br>
<br>
-Emo Philips</font></p>

